            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 73-year-old female, who presented to our emergency department on 26/08/2005 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically consistent with acute appendicitis. Diagnostic workup including physical examination, laboratory investigations, and abdominal ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis. Given her age and symptom severity, the decision was made to proceed with surgical intervention. The patient underwent a laparoscopic appendectomy on 26/08/2005 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, she received intravenous fluids, analgesics, and prophylactic antibiotics. Wound care was provided prior to discharge. Vital signs remained stable throughout her stay. She was discharged home on 27/08/2005 in satisfactory condition with wound care instructions and outpatient follow-up recommended. Please do not hesitate to contact me for further information.</div>"
            },
